For every integer $e\ge1$, $$\label{eq:nearest-integer-bound}
 H_e\le q^{d_e}\bigl(1+4q^{1-2d_e}\bigr)\le5.$$ If $1\le e<t_0$, then $$\label{eq:low-degree-distances}
 d_e=
 \begin{cases}
   se/(2n),&e\text{ even},\\
   1/2-se/(2n),&e\text{ odd},
 \end{cases}
 \qquad d_e\ge\frac{se}{2n}.$$ In this range, odd degrees satisfy $H_e\le q^{d_e/2}$, whereas even degrees satisfy $$\label{eq:even-degree-bound}
 H_e\le q^{d_e}\exp\bigl(4q^{1-se/n}\bigr).$$ There is an absolute constant $C_1$ such that, for every finite list of positive integers $e_1,\ldots,e_r$ with $\sum_j e_j\le n$, $$\label{eq:even-corrections}
 \sum_{\substack{j:\ e_j<t_0\\ e_j\text{ even}}}
 q^{1-se_j/n}\le C_1\sqrt n.$$

For all sufficiently large $n$, use the layer schedule <a href="#eq:balanced-schedule" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:balanced-schedule">[eq:balanced-schedule]</a> and the parameters $\alpha,\beta,\rho$ of <a href="#eq:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:parameters">[eq:parameters]</a>. For a word $\tau\in\{\mathcal N,\mathcal D\}^n$, let $w(\tau)$ be its number of switches, let $d_V(\tau),d_U(\tau)$ count its $\mathcal D$ positions in the respective variable groups, and let $N_V(\tau)$ be its set of $\mathcal N$ positions in group $V$. For $t\in N_V(\tau)$, let $h_t$ be the number of internal vertices adjacent to layer $t$ whose two incident layers both have type $\mathcal N$. There is an absolute constant $C$ such that $$\label{eq:word-sum}
 \sum_{\tau\in\{\mathcal N,\mathcal D\}^n}
 n^{-w(\tau)}\alpha^{-d_V(\tau)}\beta^{d_U(\tau)}
 \prod_{t\in N_V(\tau)}\bigl(1+\alpha^{-1}n^{-h_t}\bigr)
 \le \exp(C\sqrt n).$$

hypotheses.statement:
Let $L$ be an integer with $0\le L<|\mathbb F|$, choose any $L+1$ distinct elements $S_L\subseteq\mathbb F$, and set $$\omega_L(s)=\left(\prod_{u\in S_L\setminus\{s\}}(s-u)\right)^{-1}
 \qquad(s\in S_L).$$ For every univariate polynomial $p$ of degree at most $L$, $$\label{eq:top-coefficient}
 [X^L]p(X)=\sum_{s\in S_L}\omega_L(s)p(s).$$ If $Q$ is homogeneous of degree $L$, then, for arbitrary field vectors $a,v$, $$\label{eq:translation-extraction}
 \sum_{s\in S_L}\omega_L(s)Q(a+sv)=Q(v).$$ Choosing $S_L$ as the first $L+1$ elements in the uniform field enumeration, its points and weights can be enumerated and recomputed in $\operatorname{polylog}T$ space when $L$ is polynomially bounded in $T$.

evidence.quote:
Under the preceding assumptions, the augmented value of a word $V$ can be computed deterministically in $$\label{eq:weighted-evaluation-space}
 O\bigl((d+W(V)+1)\operatorname{polylog}T\bigr)$$ work space, in addition to separately stored immutable auxiliary data. More strongly, a pool of exactly $p=\max(3,r+1)$ registers in $\mathbb F^{d+1}$ supports a procedure $\mathsf{Add}(V,\lambda,t)$, for every $\lambda\in\mathbb F$ and target index $t$, with the following contract for *every* initial assignment to the entire pool: $$\label{eq:add-contract}
 R_t^{\mathrm{out}}=R_t^{\mathrm{in}}+\lambda\overline{v(V)},
 \qquad
 R_j^{\mathrm{out}}=R_j^{\mathrm{in}}\quad(j\ne t).$$ The space bound <a href="#eq:weighted-evaluation-space" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:weighted-evaluation-space">[eq:weighted-evaluation-space]</a> includes this shared pool and all temporary and suspended storage.

